"watchRatio":0.4661},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4646},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.4585},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4531},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.449},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4459},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.44049999999999995},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.4325},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4233},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.41100000000000003},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.3969},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3908}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":134,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"The world's symbol of love was built on the misery of Indians. Between 1630 and 1632, a famine tore through the Deccan. Nearly 70 lakh people starved to death. It wasn't a national disaster. Shah Jahan's army was crushing a rebellion in Malwa. They laid Deccan to the waste and burned peasants' crops. The drought did the rest and the tax did not stop. Mughal officials took more than half of every peasant's produce. Compare that to Vijayanagara rulers in the south. They took 1/6. And then they started building. In 1632, as peasants still starved in Deccan, Shah Jahan broke ground on the Taj Mahal. 22 years, 20,000 workers, funded by the same tax that emptied their villages. The world calls it greatest symbol of love. 7 million Indians paid price for it."}}